.TH CYAML_GET 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_get \- navigate to a DOM node with a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_get(cyaml root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Navigate from root through a dot-separated path and return the node at
the destination.

Path syntax:
  - A dot ('.') separates the components.
  - A component that starts with '#' followed by one or more decimal
    digits indexes into a CYAML_LIST by position; for example, "#0"
    is the first element.
  - The macro reads every other component as a literal
    CYAML_DICTIONARY key.
  - A bare '#' with no digits after it has no valid meaning as an
    index.  When the current node is a CYAML_DICTIONARY, the macro
    uses it as a literal key, exactly as it uses any other component
    that is not "#N", so such a path addresses a key with the literal
    name "#".  When the current node is a CYAML_LIST, there is no
    literal-key fallback for a list at all, so the navigation fails
    and the macro returns NULL.  cjson rejects a bare '#' in that case
    in the same way.
  - Escape sequences: '\e.' gives a literal '.' in the key instead of
    a separator, and '\e\e' gives a literal '\e' in the key.  A '\e'
    before any other character stays unchanged.
  - An empty component (nothing between two dots, a leading dot, or a
    trailing dot) has no valid meaning, either as a literal
    empty-string key or as a "#N" index.  This holds at every position
    in the path, and the navigation fails.
  - A NULL or empty path identifies root itself, and the macro then
    gives root.
  - A NULL root identifies no node, and the macro then gives NULL.

The returned reference is NON-OWNING and becomes invalid when the
caller changes or destroys the node that holds it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root  Root cyaml handle, normally a CYAML_DICTIONARY or a CYAML_LIST.
path  Dot-separated path, given as a string literal or a char *.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A non-owning cyaml handle at the target, or NULL when root is NULL, when
a component along the path is missing, when the node type does not
fit, or when the path holds an empty component.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml port = cyaml_get(doc, "server.ports.#0");
if (cyaml_type(port) == CYAML_INTEGER)
    printf("%lld\en", cyaml_int_val(port));

/* An empty path gives root itself */
cyaml same = cyaml_get(doc, "");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_set (3)
